    30	
    31	Coefficient growth cannot explain these particular failures: their arithmetic is already over a fixed prime field. The expensive objects are polynomial supports, standard-basis intermediates, syzygy modules, and msolve’s monomial tables.
    32	
    33	**Two exact reformulations shared by the difficult cases**
    34	
    35	**A. Global ideal membership and coefficient rank**
    36	
    37	For degree-\(d\) products \(P_1,\ldots,P_c\), form
    38	\[
    39	Q_d=J+\mathfrak m(P_1,\ldots,P_c).
    40	\]
    41	Then
    42	\[
    43	h_d=\dim_k\operatorname{span}\{\operatorname{NF}_{Q_d}(P_j)\}.
    44	\]
    45	The constant left kernel of their coefficient matrix supplies the degree-\(d\) relations.
    46	
    47	The justification is exact: \(((P)+J)/Q_d\) is annihilated by \(\mathfrak m\), so localization at \(\mathfrak m\) preserves it. A global `dp` computation therefore recovers the same degree-\(d\) invariant as the local syzygy computation.
    48	
    49	The existing X-minus probe already builds this quotient and completes `std(Q)` in seventeen recorded seconds. Its log separately records the subsequent reductions. The seventeen-second figure times the standard-basis stage, rather than a retained end-to-end coefficient-rank benchmark. [probe script:5–20](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pG_xm_global_d2.sing:5), [probe log:6–9](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pG_xm_global_d2.log:6)
    50	
    51	Once certified relations \(K_{\le d-1}\) exist, use
    52	`kbase(std(Kprevious),d)` as product representatives. Nakayama’s lemma justifies the reduction **locally**, which is sufficient for \(F(I)\). The retained counts give:
    53	
    54	- X: \(680\) cubic monomials reduce to \(122\).
    55	- Iso: \(969\) cubic monomials reduce to \(265\).
    56	
    57	[probes/pA_xm_rel.log:9–13](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pA_xm_rel.log:9), [probes/pD_iso.log:3–6](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pD_iso.log:3)
    58	
    59	**B. Pivot saturation for the entire Rees ideal**
    60	
    61	Choose \(g=G_q\) that is a nonzerodivisor modulo \(J\). In \(k[t,y]\), define
    62	\[
    63	H=J+(g y_j-G_jy_q:j\ne q).
    64	\]
    65	Then
    66	\[
    67	\mathcal R(I)=k[t,y]/(H:g^\infty).
    68	\]
    69	
    70	After inverting \(g\), the equations express every \(y_j\) through \(y_q\). The nonzerodivisor condition makes contraction recover the Rees kernel. Thus
    71	\[
    72	F(I)=k[t,y]\big/\big((H:g^\infty)+(t)\big).
    73	\]
    74	
    75	This formulation avoids explicitly computing the large local syzygy module and avoids presenting the graph through \(a\). Saturation can still be expensive, but a completed result supplies the **full** fiber ideal and Hilbert series.
    76	
    77	The installed Macaulay2 implementation exposes the same saturation mechanism and explicitly requires a suitable nonzerodivisor. [ReesAlgebra.m2:146–161](/usr/share/Macaulay2/ReesAlgebra.m2:146)
    78	
    79	**1. X-plus → N-plus**
    80	
    81	The strongest available reduction is an exact symmetry of the supplied inputs.
    82	
    83	I checked every printed coefficient under
    84	\[
    85	\sigma(t_1,t_2,t_4,t_5,t_6,t_7,t_9)
    86	=(t_1,t_2,t_4,t_5,t_6,t_7,-t_9).
    87	\]
    88	The identities over \(\mathbb F_{32009}\) are
    89	\[
    90	J^-_j=\epsilon_j\,J^+_j\circ\sigma,\qquad
    91	\epsilon=(+,-,-,-,-,-,-,-,+,+,+,+),
    92	\]
    93	and
    94	\[
    95	G^-_j=\delta_j\,G^+_j\circ\sigma,\qquad
    96	\delta=(+,+,+,+,+,+,-,-,-,-,-,-,-,-,-).
    97	\]
    98	These identities use the supplied generator order. [positive input:4–5](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/kaggle-pack-a/xp_np/input.sing:4), [negative input:4–5](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/kaggle-pack-a/xm_nm/input.sing:4)
    99	
   100	Consequently, the two recorded fiber algebras are isomorphic, with target substitution
   101	\[
   102	y_j\longmapsto\delta_jy_j.
   103	\]
   104	
   105	Ranked options:
