   427	**Sparse linear algebra can provide a rigorous stopping rule**
   428	
   429	Two finite matrix constructions give complementary bounds on each \(h_d\):
   430	
   431	1. **Full-polynomial cofactor search.** Include bounded-degree monomial multiples of \(Q_d\), retaining every resulting coefficient. Relations found this way are exact. Increasing the cofactor bound produces decreasing upper bounds \(h_{\mathrm{upper}}\).
   432	2. **Jet quotient.** Compute modulo \(Q_d+\mathfrak m^N\). This produces increasing lower bounds \(h_{\mathrm{lower}}\).
   433	
   434	Stop when
   435	\[
   436	h_{\mathrm{lower}}=h_{\mathrm{upper}}.
   437	\]
   438	That equality certifies the degree-\(d\) answer. Two unchanged ranks alone supply no such certificate.
   439	
   440	FLINT suits modest dense coefficient matrices. LinBox suits large sparse matrices and block Wiedemann experiments. GPU acceleration becomes relevant after measuring matrix dimensions, nonzero counts, and modular-operation costs. A probabilistic rank candidate needs verification sufficient to certify the rank; a floating-point rank is insufficient.
   441	
   442	This route preserves each certified graded piece. Computing finitely many pieces still requires a separate argument for the full exceptional fiber.
   443	
   444	**Critique of `first-principles-plan.md`**
   445	
   446	The plan correctly identifies global membership, normal-monomial reduction, and the inexpensive X-minus support reduction. I would change the following claims before using the plan to close the research obligation:
   447	
   448	1. **Remove the proposed regularity shortcut.** Regularity of \(k[y]/K_2\) does not bound the degree of an additional relation in the unknown quotient \(F(I)\). Even a polynomial ring of regularity zero can acquire a new relation in arbitrarily high degree. The claim at line 29 therefore supplies no completion certificate. [plan:29](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/first-principles-plan.md:29)
   449	2. **Update the msolve diagnosis with the cgroup runs.** The measurements distinguish address-space failures from later physical-memory exhaustion. The universal conclusion “msolve is not the tool” exceeds those measurements; its present graph formulation is a low-priority route. [plan:31–34](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/first-principles-plan.md:31), [measured rows:167–168](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/runbooks/compute-flags.md:167)
   450	3. **Locate the cubic failure before estimating its next cost.** The retained probe dies before its product-summary marker. The suggested 8–24-GiB requirement remains an estimate, and early product reduction deserves the first experiment. [plan:18](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/first-principles-plan.md:18), [pH script:14–18](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/probes/pH_xm_global_d3.sing:14)
   451	4. **Restrict the irreducibility statement to the computed field.** One minimal prime over \(\mathbb F_{32009}\) establishes arithmetic irreducibility there. Geometric irreducibility and characteristic-zero irreducibility require further evidence. [plan:22](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/first-principles-plan.md:22)
   452	5. **Treat finite-field sections as diagnostics.** Univariate factors describe Frobenius orbits of section points. They do not directly give geometric component degrees; multiplicities and transversality also require checks. [plan:25](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/first-principles-plan.md:25)
   453	6. **Separate multigrading, representation decomposition, and primary decomposition.** A torus grading can split a calculation into blocks. It neither forces toric components nor makes primary decomposition cheap, and weight spaces differ from irreducible components. [plan:27](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/first-principles-plan.md:27)
   454	7. **Replace the universal dimension-three bound and the one-point transport assumption.** The source dimensions give the bounds listed above. Moreover, base-orbit transitivity alone does not transport kernels: the repository explicitly records that the split boost has no kernel lift. [plan:52–55](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/first-principles-plan.md:52), [program:23](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/design/a5-kernel-bundles-nullity8-program.md:23)
   455	8. **Correct W8’s product count:** 24 generators give \(300\) quadratic products, rather than \(325\). [plan:12](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/first-principles-plan.md:12), [W8 input:8–10](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/inputs/w8_nm_fiber.sing:8)
   456	
   457	The sister analysis also proposes `DegreeLimit` for Macaulay2 Rees computation. The installed implementation explicitly documents that this option has no effect in `reesIdeal`; it must not be used as a resource or completeness guarantee. [algebra-options.md:29](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-iso-np-h3-analysis/algebra-options.md:29), [ReesAlgebra.m2:113–122](/usr/share/Macaulay2/ReesAlgebra.m2:113)
