 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Compute flags: memory caps, threads and solver options
     2	
     3	This runbook is the flag review that precedes every command whose cost or
     4	correctness depends on its options: computer algebra (Singular, msolve,
     5	Macaulay2), simulators (Aer, cuStateVec), Python pools, compilers, CI tools,
     6	cloud launchers and resource limiters, on the build host, Azure VMs, Kaggle
     7	kernels or any other substrate. The tool sections below cover the tools
     8	measured so far; a tool without a section gets one the first time it runs long. An agent or operator fills in the
     9	checklist at the end before the run starts and records the filled checklist
    10	beside the run's log. A run launched without it gets stopped and relaunched.
    11	
    12	[library-flags.md](library-flags.md) is the per-library companion: the environment
    13	variables, API options and build facts of every package the repository imports.
    14	
    15	## Why the review exists
    16	
    17	Every msolve run on the A_5 Rees-fiber eliminations died with exit 139 under a
    18	`prlimit --as` cap while its resident set sat far below that cap:
    19	
    20	| Cap | msolve flags | Death | Peak resident set |
    21	|---|---|---|---|
    22	| `prlimit --as` 80 GiB | `-t 4 -e 1 -g 2` | degree 7, "Allocating memory for hash-column lookup table failed" | 33.6 GB |
    23	| `prlimit --as` 28 GiB | `-t 3 -m 200000 -s 24 -l 1 -e 1 -g 2` | degree 6, "Enlarging exponent vector for hash table failed for esz = 268435456" | 28.0 GB |
    24	| `prlimit --as` 28 GiB | `-t 3 -s 24 -u 2 -e 1 -g 2` | same point, same message | 18.1 GB |
    25	
    26	`RLIMIT_AS`, which `prlimit --as` and Python's
    27	`resource.setrlimit(RLIMIT_AS, ...)` set, limits reserved virtual address
    28	space, not resident memory. The msolve 0.10.1 source names the two failing
    29	allocations: `enlarge_hash_table` in `src/neogb/hash.c` reallocates the whole
    30	exponent block at each doubling (`esz * evl * sizeof(exp_t)` bytes, with the
    31	old block still mapped until `realloc` returns), and `src/neogb/convert.c`
    32	reallocates the hash-to-column map (`(esld - 1) * sizeof(hi_t)` bytes) once per
    33	matrix. glibc also reserves one malloc arena per OpenMP thread. The address
    34	space therefore runs far ahead of the resident set, and the cap refuses an
    35	allocation that physical memory could serve, so the failure looked like a
    36	fixed internal limit. Several debugging rounds, including a consultation of
    37	five frontier models, went into msolve internals before reading the cap
    38	itself. The confirming run under a cgroup memory cap with the address-space
    39	limit lifted is recorded in the A_5 attachment evidence once it ends; until
    40	then the address-space explanation is the leading one, not a measured fact.
    41	
    42	The general rule: a resource limit, a default thread count, or an unexamined
    43	solver default can masquerade as a property of the mathematics. Read every
    44	flag of the launch line before blaming the algorithm.
    45	
    46	## Memory caps
    47	
    48	Every mechanism below is available; the review picks one memory limit and one
    49	time limit per run from what the substrate supports and records why.
    50	
    51	| Mechanism | What it limits | When it fits | Caveat |
    52	|---|---|---|---|
    53	| cgroup v2 `MemoryMax` (`systemd-run --user --scope -p MemoryMax=NG -p MemorySwapMax=0 CMD`) | resident memory plus page cache charged to the job; the kernel OOM-kills the job alone at the limit | default on the build host and Azure VMs | needs the `memory` controller delegated to the user manager (`cat /sys/fs/cgroup/user.slice/user-$(id -u).slice/user@$(id -u).service/cgroup.controllers`) and lingering (`loginctl enable-linger USER`) so the scope survives logout |
    54	| cgroup v2 `MemoryHigh` (same command, `-p MemoryHigh=NG`) | soft limit: the kernel reclaims and throttles above it instead of killing | pairing with `MemoryMax` to slow a job before it dies | throttling can stretch wall time severalfold |
    55	| container limit (`docker run --memory`, Kaggle's own 32 GB) | the container's cgroup | runs that already sit in a container | the whole container dies, every job in it included |
    56	| RSS watchdog (sample `VmRSS` of the job and its children, kill above N) | resident memory, at sample granularity | Kaggle kernels and other containers without a delegated cgroup; the multi-job template already samples every 30 s | a fast spike between samples escapes it |
    57	| `prlimit --as=BYTES CMD`, `ulimit -v`, `resource.setrlimit(RLIMIT_AS, ...)` | virtual address space reserved by the process | single-threaded programs whose virtual-to-resident ratio was measured small | refuses allocations far below physical memory for msolve, OpenMP programs, JIT runtimes and anything that reserves before touching |
    58	| `prlimit --rss`, `RLIMIT_RSS` | nothing on Linux since 2.6 | never | accepted and ignored by the kernel |
    59	| `prlimit --data`, `RLIMIT_DATA` | heap and private anonymous mappings | rarely; counts reservations like `--as` | same caveat as `--as` |
    60	| `timeout -k 10 SECONDS CMD` | wall time | every run, beside one memory limit | |
    61	| `systemd-run -p RuntimeMaxSec=` | wall time of the scope or service | runs already under `systemd-run` | |
    62	
    63	Set `MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=2` in the environment of every multithreaded C program
    64	so glibc keeps two arenas instead of one per thread; this shrinks address-space
    65	growth and fragmentation at no measured cost to these workloads.
