     8	  Headline
     9	    whether certain Groebner computations can make use of the Hilbert function
    10	  Usage
    11	    canUseHilbertHint m
    12	  Inputs
    13	    m:{Ring,Ideal,Module,Matrix}
    14	  Outputs
    15	    :Boolean
    16	  Description
    17	    Text
    18	      Certain Groebner basis computations, when given homogeneous input, can run faster if
    19	      the Hilbert function is know: the algorithm can use this information to bypass
    20	      computing zero reductions in many cases.
    21	
    22	      In order to be able to use a Hilbert function hint, currently the object must be
    23	      in a singly graded polynomial ring (or quotient of such), over a field,
    24	      with all generators of positive degree, and it must be either commutative, or skew-commutative.
    25	
    26	      Given a ring, this function returns whether this holds for the ring.  For
    27	      a module, matrix or ideal, this function additionally checks whether the input is
    28	      homogeneous.
    29	    Example
    30	      R1 = ZZ/101[x,y,z, Degrees => {1,2,3}];
    31	      assert canUseHilbertHint R1
    32	      R2 = ZZ[x,y,z];
    33	      assert not canUseHilbertHint R2
    34	      R3 = (GF 8)[x,y,z]
    35	      assert canUseHilbertHint R3
    36	      use R1
    37	      R4 = R1[u,v, Join => false]/(x*u+y*v)
    38	      R5 = first flattenRing R4
    39	      canUseHilbertHint R4
    40	      canUseHilbertHint R5
    41	    Text
    42	      Here is one way to use Hilbert hints. If one knows the Hilbert function, one can use it.
    43	      If one computes a Hilbert function (via poincare), one can use that in a ring
    44	      which is identical, except the monomial order is more computationally intensive (e.g. Lex).
    45	    Example
    46	      R = ZZ/101[x,y,z,w]
    47	      I = ideal random(R^1, R^{-5,-5,-5})
    48	      hf = poincare I
    49	      codim I == 3 -- a complete intersection
    50	      Rlex = newRing(R, MonomialOrder => Lex)
    51	      Ilex = sub(I, Rlex)
    52	      elapsedTime g1 = gens gb Ilex;
    53	      Ilex = ideal(Ilex_*) -- clear out the previous Groebner basis
    54	      elapsedTime g2 = gens gb(Ilex, Hilbert => hf);
    55	      g1 == g2
    56	  Caveat
    57	    One cannot currently use Hilbert hints for multigraded input
    58	  SeeAlso
    59	    poincare
    60	    newRing
    61	///
